# LIGHT desktop package set — Raspberry Pi 5 (BCM2712, aarch64)
#
# A LEAN desktop, NOT the full Plasma+gaming stage4. Appended to packages.list by
# build-rpi5-stage4.sh when --role desktop is set (ROLE_CLIENT — no k8s stack).
# Sized for the Pi 5's VideoCore VII (v3d/vc4 KMS driver, already unconditional
# in make.conf's VIDEO_CARDS): X11 + a lightweight WM + a panel + a file manager +
# a terminal + a minimal browser. No KDE/GNOME/Plasma, no compositor-heavy
# Wayland session, no gaming stack. Mirrors specs-frite/portage/packages-light-desktop.list
# and specs-jetson/portage/packages-desktop.list.

# ── Display server (X11; lean) ─────────────────────────────────────
x11-base/xorg-server
# Materializes the X drivers from INPUT_DEVICES=libinput + VIDEO_CARDS="v3d vc4"
# (already unconditional in specs-rpi5/portage/make.conf.tmpl); without it Xorg
# has no input/video driver and the greeter is unusable.
x11-base/xorg-drivers
x11-apps/xrandr
x11-apps/xset
x11-apps/setxkbmap
x11-terms/xterm

# ── Desktop session: LXDE-lite (PCManFM + Openbox + LXpanel) ───────
# LXDE is the canonical "lean desktop": GTK-based, no compositor required, runs
# comfortably on the Pi 5. Openbox is the WM; lxpanel the panel; pcmanfm the
# file manager; lxsession ties the session together.
x11-wm/openbox
lxde-base/lxpanel
lxde-base/lxsession
x11-misc/pcmanfm
lxde-base/lxappearance

# ── Login / session bring-up ───────────────────────────────────────
# LightDM is the lean display manager; on a single-user appliance you can also
# autostart Openbox from .xinitrc and drop lightdm — see RUNNING.md.
x11-misc/lightdm
x11-misc/lightdm-gtk-greeter
# The OpenRC `display-manager` init script does NOT ship with lightdm itself —
# it is provided by gui-libs/display-manager-init (configured via
# /etc/conf.d/display-manager DISPLAYMANAGER="lightdm", written by the builder).
# Without this the builder would enable a service the image never provides and
# the desktop image would boot to a bare console.
gui-libs/display-manager-init

# ── Desktop-governance runtime (SSO `desktop-sudo` claim -> polkit) ─────
# The SAME runtime specs-x86/portage/packages-desktop.list declares under
# "SSO-claim sudo runtime" (full rationale there + docs/CLIENT-IMAGES.md).
# keys-provision's provision_desktop_governance() is shared by every image:
# on a GOVERNED client (templates baked or on the KEYS USB) it renders the
# default-deny polkit rules.d gate and the stdlib-only python mapper cron, and
# strips sudoers/%wheel — so polkit becomes the ONLY escalation route. Both
# runtimes are declared here so that route is a stated contract of this list,
# not an accident of the dependency graph:
#   sys-auth/polkit  — today arrives only through lxde-base/lxsession's
#                      COMMON_DEPEND; a session-manager swap must not silently
#                      take the governed image's sole sudo path with it. It must
#                      be built with elogind session tracking (the ebuild's
#                      ConsoleKit fallback sees no sessions) — elogind is in the builder's
#                      CLIENT_USE append for --role desktop.
#   dev-lang/python  — the mapper's interpreter; present through portage anyway,
#                      declared for the same reason as on x86.
# The mapper's 60s floor engine (sys-process/cronie) is in packages.list and in
# the builder's DEFAULT_SVCS. Pinned by tests/scripts/test-arm-desktop-package-sets.sh.
sys-auth/polkit
dev-lang/python

# DELIBERATELY NOT gnome-extra/nm-applet (the x86 list's graphical NM front-end):
# its hard RDEPEND net-libs/libnma needs gui-libs/gtk-4 UNCONDITIONALLY — the
# exact chain the per-board package.use already pins libfm[-automount] and
# pinentry[-gtk] off for, because gtk-4 fails to cross-configure — and its
# +modemmanager default USE-requires networkmanager[modemmanager], which every
# ARM spec forces off. Network configuration on this desktop is nmtui/nmcli
# (networkmanager[+tools]) from x11-terms/xterm. Tracked as a separate issue.

# ── Minimal apps ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
media-gfx/feh
app-editors/nano
# A minimal, modern browser. NetSurf cross-compiles cleanly for arm64 and needs
# no GPU acceleration.
www-client/netsurf

# ── Fonts (so the lean desktop is legible out of the box) ──────────
media-fonts/dejavu
media-fonts/noto
